window.PE_SEED = {"students": [], "plans": {}, "tests": [{"id": "rd-B2-01", "title": "Czytanka 1: The quiet ones", "level": "B2", "topic": "Człowiek", "subject": "ang", "instructions": "Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, zgodną z treścią tekstu. Po oddaniu zobaczysz wyjaśnienia.", "passages": [{"title": "The quiet ones", "text": "For years, teachers have praised students who speak up in class. Answering quickly, joining every discussion and volunteering for presentations were often seen as signs of intelligence and confidence. Quiet students, on the other hand, were sometimes described in school reports as 'shy' or 'not engaged'. But research into personality suggests that this picture is not entirely fair.\n\nPsychologists use the word 'introvert' to describe people who feel more comfortable in calm environments with fewer people and less noise. Introverts are not necessarily shy. Shyness is a fear of being judged by others, while introversion is simply a preference. An introvert may enjoy talking to friends, but after a long day with a lot of people, he or she needs time alone to recharge.\n\nIn the classroom, introverts often think carefully before they answer. By the time they have prepared a response, the discussion may have moved on. As a result, teachers sometimes assume they have nothing to say. Emma, a 17-year-old student from Leeds, describes it like this: 'I always knew the answer, but someone louder said it first. After a while, I just stopped trying.'\n\nSome schools are now changing the way they run lessons. Instead of asking the whole class a question and choosing the first raised hand, teachers give everyone a minute to write down their ideas. Students then discuss their answers in pairs before sharing them with the group. These small changes give quieter students time to think, and they also improve the quality of the discussion for everybody.\n\nOf course, introverts also need to learn to speak in public, because it is an important skill at university and at work. However, experts agree that the goal should not be to turn quiet people into loud ones. Instead, schools should help every student find a way of taking part that suits their personality."}], "questions": [{"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "According to the first paragraph, quiet students were often", "o": ["considered less involved in lessons.", "praised for their careful work.", "given more difficult tasks.", "encouraged to give presentations."], "c": 0, "e": "Akapit 1: w opiniach szkolnych cichych uczniów opisywano jako „shy” albo „not engaged”, czyli mniej zaangażowanych. Pochwały (B) i prezentacje (D) dotyczyły uczniów, którzy dużo mówią."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "What is the difference between shyness and introversion?", "o": ["Shy people never enjoy talking to friends.", "Introversion is a preference, while shyness is a kind of fear.", "Introverts are afraid of being judged by others.", "Shyness disappears after spending time alone."], "c": 1, "e": "Akapit 2: „Shyness is a fear of being judged… while introversion is simply a preference”. Odpowiedź C to pułapka: strach przed oceną to cecha nieśmiałości, nie introwersji."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "Why do teachers sometimes think that introverts have nothing to say?", "o": ["They rarely know the right answers.", "They prefer to work alone.", "They need more time to prepare an answer.", "They do not listen to the discussion."], "c": 2, "e": "Akapit 3: introwertycy długo zastanawiają się nad odpowiedzią, a zanim są gotowi, dyskusja idzie dalej („By the time they have prepared a response, the discussion may have moved on”)."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "Emma's words show that she", "o": ["was not interested in the lessons.", "gave up because other students answered before her.", "was afraid of her teacher.", "did not understand the questions."], "c": 1, "e": "Emma znała odpowiedź, ale ktoś głośniejszy mówił ją pierwszy, więc w końcu przestała próbować („I just stopped trying”). Zwrot „give up” = przestać próbować."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The changes described in paragraph 4", "o": ["are useful only for quiet students.", "make lessons longer and more tiring.", "improve the discussion for the whole class.", "replace group discussions completely."], "c": 2, "e": "Ostatnie zdanie akapitu 4: zmiany dają cichym uczniom czas do namysłu i poprawiają jakość dyskusji „for everybody”. Dlatego A jest za wąskie, a D nieprawdziwe – dyskusja w grupie zostaje."}, {"type": "tf", "p": 0, "t": "Experts believe that schools should try to make introverts more talkative.", "c": false, "e": "Fałsz. Ostatni akapit: „the goal should not be to turn quiet people into loud ones”."}, {"type": "tf", "p": 0, "t": "The author thinks that public speaking is a useful skill for introverts too.", "c": true, "e": "Prawda. Ostatni akapit: introwertycy też muszą nauczyć się mówić publicznie, bo to ważna umiejętność na studiach i w pracy."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The main purpose of the text is to", "o": ["criticise teachers who prefer loud students.", "explain why quiet students may be misjudged and how schools can help.", "give shy people advice on how to make friends.", "describe the life of a student from Leeds."], "c": 1, "e": "Tekst wyjaśnia, dlaczego cisi uczniowie bywają źle oceniani (akapity 1–3), i pokazuje, jak szkoły mogą im pomóc (akapity 4–5). A jest za mocne – autor nie krytykuje nauczycieli, a historia Emmy (D) to tylko przykład."}]}, {"id": "rd-B2P-01", "title": "Czytanka 1: A tenth of a second", "level": "B2+", "topic": "Człowiek", "subject": "ang", "instructions": "Zadania 1–4: dopasuj informacje do akapitów A–D. Zadania 5–8: wybierz odpowiedź zgodną z treścią tekstu.", "passages": [{"title": "A tenth of a second", "text": "[A] It takes about a tenth of a second. In one well-known experiment, psychologists showed volunteers photographs of strangers' faces for just 100 milliseconds and asked them to rate how trustworthy, competent or friendly each person seemed. Remarkably, the judgements made in that instant were almost identical to the ones people made when they were given as much time as they wanted. Longer exposure did not change their minds; it simply made them more confident that they were right.\n\n[B] Why are we so quick to judge? Evolutionary psychologists argue that for our ancestors, deciding instantly whether a stranger was a friend or a threat could be a matter of life and death. Those who hesitated may not have survived long enough to pass on their genes. The problem is that the mental shortcuts which once protected us were not designed for job interviews, first dates or classrooms.\n\n[C] And these shortcuts are far from reliable. People with round faces and large eyes, for example, tend to be seen as honest and naive, whatever their real character. Some studies of court cases have even suggested that defendants who 'look trustworthy' are more likely to receive lighter sentences. In other words, a face we had no say in choosing can shape the way the world treats us.\n\n[D] The good news is that first impressions are not set in stone. When we get to know someone, real information gradually replaces our assumptions – but only if we give it a chance. Researchers recommend a simple habit: before forming an opinion about a new classmate or colleague, remind yourself that what you have seen is a snapshot, not the whole film. It sounds obvious, yet it is surprisingly easy to forget."}], "questions": [{"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "W którym akapicie autor wyjaśnia, skąd wzięła się nasza skłonność do szybkiego oceniania?", "o": ["Akapit A", "Akapit B", "Akapit C", "Akapit D"], "c": 1, "e": "Akapit B: wyjaśnienie ewolucyjne – u przodków szybka decyzja „przyjaciel czy wróg” mogła ratować życie."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "W którym akapicie autor podaje przykład tego, że ocena czyjegoś wyglądu może mieć poważne konsekwencje?", "o": ["Akapit A", "Akapit B", "Akapit C", "Akapit D"], "c": 2, "e": "Akapit C: oskarżeni, którzy „wyglądają na godnych zaufania”, częściej dostają łagodniejsze wyroki. To poważna konsekwencja oceny po twarzy."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "W którym akapicie autor opisuje badanie, w którym dodatkowy czas nie zmienił opinii uczestników?", "o": ["Akapit A", "Akapit B", "Akapit C", "Akapit D"], "c": 0, "e": "Akapit A: „Longer exposure did not change their minds” – dłuższe oglądanie zdjęć nie zmieniło ocen."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "W którym akapicie autor radzi, jak ograniczyć wpływ pierwszego wrażenia?", "o": ["Akapit A", "Akapit B", "Akapit C", "Akapit D"], "c": 3, "e": "Akapit D: badacze polecają prosty nawyk – przypomnieć sobie, że widzieliśmy tylko „a snapshot, not the whole film”."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "According to paragraph A, when the volunteers had more time to look at the faces,", "o": ["they changed most of their opinions.", "they felt more certain about their first judgements.", "they found the strangers more trustworthy.", "their ratings became more accurate."], "c": 1, "e": "„It simply made them more confident that they were right” – byli bardziej pewni swoich ocen. D to pułapka: pewność ≠ trafność, a tekst nic nie mówi o tym, że oceny były dokładniejsze."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The word 'shortcuts' in paragraph B refers to", "o": ["quick ways of making decisions without much thought.", "faster routes used by our ancestors.", "mistakes made during job interviews.", "genes passed on to future generations."], "c": 0, "e": "„Mental shortcuts” to w tym kontekście szybkie, automatyczne sposoby oceniania. B to dosłowne znaczenie „shortcut” (skrót drogi) – typowa pułapka."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "In paragraph C, the author suggests that", "o": ["people with round faces are usually honest.", "judges should not look at defendants.", "our appearance may cause others to treat us unfairly.", "only naive people trust faces."], "c": 2, "e": "Ostatnie zdanie akapitu C: twarz, której sobie nie wybraliśmy, może wpływać na to, jak traktuje nas świat. A jest błędne – tekst mówi, że takie osoby są tak POSTRZEGANE, niezależnie od prawdziwego charakteru."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The phrase 'a snapshot, not the whole film' means that", "o": ["photographs are more reliable than films.", "a first impression shows only a small part of a person.", "we should observe people for a long time before meeting them.", "new classmates often hide their real personality."], "c": 1, "e": "Metafora: pierwsze wrażenie to jedna klatka (snapshot), a nie cały człowiek (whole film). Pozostałe odpowiedzi biorą słowa z tekstu, ale zmieniają sens."}]}, {"id": "rd-C1-02", "title": "Czytanka 1: Who do you think you are?", "level": "C1", "topic": "Człowiek", "subject": "ang", "instructions": "Zadania 1–3: wybierz zdanie (A–D), które pasuje do luki. Jedno zdanie nie pasuje do żadnej luki. Zadania 4–8: wybierz odpowiedź zgodną z treścią tekstu.", "passages": [{"title": "Who do you think you are?", "text": "Few things are as tempting as a test that promises to tell you who you really are. Every year, millions of job applicants, students and curious internet users answer dozens of questions in the hope of discovering their 'type'. The most famous of these tools sorts people into sixteen categories, each with its own neat four-letter label. (1) ______ Yet among academic psychologists, its reputation is surprisingly poor.\n\nThe central objection concerns the way such tests divide people into boxes. Most personality traits, researchers point out, are distributed along a continuum, much like height. Very few people are extremely outgoing or extremely reserved; the vast majority sit somewhere in the middle. (2) ______ Two people who differ by a single point may end up with opposite labels, while two whose scores are miles apart may share the same one.\n\nA second problem is reliability. If a test genuinely measures something stable, you would expect to get the same result when you take it again a few weeks later. However, studies have found that a considerable proportion of people are assigned a different type on a second attempt. A tape measure that gave you a new height every month would hardly inspire confidence.\n\nNone of this means that personality cannot be measured at all. The model favoured by most researchers, known as the Big Five, describes people in terms of five broad traits, such as openness to experience and conscientiousness, and gives each person a score on each dimension rather than a category. (3) ______ Its results, unlike those of type-based tests, have been shown to predict things like academic performance and job satisfaction, at least to a modest degree.\n\nWhy, then, do the four-letter labels remain so popular? Part of the answer is that they are flattering: every type is described in positive terms, so nobody leaves disappointed. Another part is that a label offers a sense of identity and belonging, especially online, where people proudly display their type in their profiles. The tests may tell us little about who we are, but they tell us a great deal about how much we want to be told."}], "questions": [{"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "Które zdanie pasuje do luki (1)?", "o": ["It is less exciting than a label, but it is far closer to how people actually differ.", "Forcing them into one of two categories inevitably exaggerates small differences.", "Its fans include several large companies, which use it to build teams and train managers.", "As a result, most psychologists now recommend taking the test at least twice a year."], "c": 2, "e": "Po luce jest „Yet among academic psychologists, its reputation is surprisingly poor” – „Yet” wymaga kontrastu: test ma fanów (duże firmy), ALE psychologowie oceniają go źle. „Its” w zdaniu C odnosi się do „the most famous of these tools”."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "Które zdanie pasuje do luki (2)?", "o": ["It is less exciting than a label, but it is far closer to how people actually differ.", "Forcing them into one of two categories inevitably exaggerates small differences.", "Its fans include several large companies, which use it to build teams and train managers.", "As a result, most psychologists now recommend taking the test at least twice a year."], "c": 1, "e": "Przed luką: większość ludzi jest „somewhere in the middle”. Zdanie B mówi, że wciskanie ich w jedną z dwóch kategorii wyolbrzymia małe różnice – i dokładnie to ilustruje następne zdanie (jeden punkt różnicy = przeciwne etykiety). „Them” = „the vast majority”."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "Które zdanie pasuje do luki (3)?", "o": ["It is less exciting than a label, but it is far closer to how people actually differ.", "Forcing them into one of two categories inevitably exaggerates small differences.", "Its fans include several large companies, which use it to build teams and train managers.", "As a result, most psychologists now recommend taking the test at least twice a year."], "c": 0, "e": "Przed luką: Big Five daje wynik na skali „rather than a category”. Zdanie A porównuje to z etykietą („less exciting than a label”). Zdanie D nie pasuje nigdzie – tekst nigdzie nie zaleca powtarzania testu."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The comparison with height in paragraph 2 is used to show that", "o": ["tall people tend to be more outgoing.", "personality traits vary gradually rather than falling into clear groups.", "personality, like height, does not change over time.", "extreme personalities are more common than we think."], "c": 1, "e": "„Distributed along a continuum, much like height” – cechy rozkładają się płynnie, jak wzrost, a nie w wyraźnych grupach. C to pułapka: porównanie dotyczy rozkładu, nie stałości w czasie."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The 'tape measure' in paragraph 3 illustrates the problem of", "o": ["tests that give inconsistent results.", "people who are dishonest about themselves.", "tests that are too difficult to complete.", "results that are difficult to interpret."], "c": 0, "e": "Akapit 3 dotyczy rzetelności (reliability): ten sam człowiek dostaje inny typ przy drugim podejściu. Miarka, która co miesiąc pokazuje inny wzrost, to obraz niespójnych wyników."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "According to the text, the Big Five model", "o": ["sorts people into five personality types.", "is more popular online than type-based tests.", "has some proven ability to predict real-life outcomes.", "was designed to replace job interviews."], "c": 2, "e": "„Have been shown to predict things like academic performance and job satisfaction, at least to a modest degree”. A to pułapka: Big Five daje wynik na każdej z pięciu skal, a NIE pięć typów."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "In the final paragraph, the author suggests that type-based tests are popular mainly because", "o": ["they are more accurate than people believe.", "they satisfy people's emotional needs.", "companies force employees to take them.", "they are short and easy to complete."], "c": 1, "e": "Dwa powody z ostatniego akapitu: są pochlebne („flattering”) i dają poczucie tożsamości i przynależności. Oba to potrzeby emocjonalne."}, {"type": "abcd", "p": 0, "t": "The author's attitude towards type-based personality tests is best described as", "o": ["enthusiastic.", "sceptical.", "indifferent.", "openly hostile."], "c": 1, "e": "Autor przytacza zarzuty naukowców i kończy ironicznie, że testy mówią więcej o naszej potrzebie bycia opisanym niż o nas – to sceptycyzm. Nie jest to otwarta wrogość (D): autor nie atakuje ludzi, którzy z nich korzystają, i przyznaje, że osobowość da się mierzyć."}]}], "materials": [{"id": "z01", "no": 1, "title": "Zestaw 01: Człowiek · tekst z lukami", "category": "ang-pr", "topic": "Człowiek · charakter, wygląd, uczucia · czasy przeszłe · czytanie", "body": "Dział: **Człowiek** (charakter, wygląd, uczucia) · środki językowe · czasy przeszłe · czytanie. Zadania 1–14 to część podstawowa, zadania 15–20 to część dla szybszych.\n\n## Część podstawowa\n\n**Zadanie 1.** Utwórz przeczenie, dodając przedrostek **un-, dis-, in-, im-, il-** albo **ir-**.\n\n1. patient → ________\n2. honest → ________\n3. reliable → ________\n4. responsible → ________\n5. sensitive → ________\n6. mature → ________\n7. loyal → ________\n8. considerate → ________\n\n**Zadanie 2.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrażeniami z ramki w odpowiedniej formie. Jedno wyrażenie na zdanie.\n\n> take after · put up with · bring up · get on with · lose one's temper · look up to\n\n1. Tom really ________ his father – they have the same sense of humour.\n2. I don't know how you ________ such a noisy flatmate!\n3. She was ________ by her grandparents after her parents moved abroad.\n4. My sister and I ________ really well – we never argue.\n5. When the manager heard the news, he ________ and started shouting.\n6. Many teenagers ________ famous athletes and want to be like them.\n\n**Zadanie 3.** Dopasuj przymiotniki z ramki do definicji.\n\n> stubborn · generous · reliable · arrogant · sensible · outgoing\n\n1. always ready to give money, time or help to others → ________\n2. refusing to change your opinion, even when you are wrong → ________\n3. thinking you are better or more important than other people → ________\n4. friendly and happy to meet new people → ________\n5. someone you can trust to do what they promised → ________\n6. able to make good, practical decisions → ________\n\n**Zadanie 4.** Podkreśl właściwe słowo.\n\n1. She cries at every sad film – she's very *sensible / sensitive*.\n2. It was a *sensible / sensitive* decision to save some money first.\n3. I felt really *embarrassed / embarrassing* when I fell over in front of everyone.\n4. The lecture was so *bored / boring* that half of the students fell asleep.\n5. He's *ashamed / shy* and hates speaking in front of the class.\n6. My grandfather is very *wise / clever* – he always gives good advice about life.\n\n**Zadanie 5.** Uzupełnij kolokacje czasownikami **make, take, keep** albo **lose** w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. Don't ________ your temper – just count to ten.\n2. She always ________ her promises, so you can trust her.\n3. He finds it easy to ________ friends wherever he goes.\n4. You need to ________ responsibility for your mistakes.\n5. Try to ________ calm during the exam.\n6. If you want to pass, you'll have to ________ more effort.\n\n**Zadanie 6.** Uzupełnij opisy wyglądu słowami z ramki.\n\n> bald · freckles · in her twenties · well-built · wrinkles · curly\n\n1. My uncle lost all his hair years ago – he's completely ________.\n2. She has ________ red hair which is impossible to brush.\n3. He goes to the gym every day, so he's tall and ________.\n4. My new teacher is quite young – she's probably ________.\n5. In summer, the sun gives me lots of ________ on my nose.\n6. My grandmother has deep ________ around her eyes because she smiles a lot.\n\n**Zadanie 7.** Uzupełnij zdania właściwymi przyimkami.\n\n1. My parents are really proud ________ my exam results.\n2. She has always been afraid ________ spiders.\n3. I'm fed up ________ doing the same exercises every day.\n4. Are you interested ________ psychology?\n5. He's very keen ________ playing chess.\n6. My little brother is jealous ________ my new phone.\n\n**Zadanie 8.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, tak aby otrzymać logiczny i gramatycznie poprawny tekst.\n\n> Last year my cousin Ania **(1)** ______ to London to study psychology. Before she left, everyone **(2)** ______ her she was too shy to cope on her own. She had never **(3)** ______ abroad without her parents. However, she has completely changed since then. She **(4)** ______ a lot of new friends and she even works part-time in a café. Ania says that the hardest thing was to **(5)** ______ her fear of speaking English in public. Now she is so self-confident that her old friends can hardly **(6)** ______ her. 'I **(7)** ______ about going back to Poland after graduation,' she told me last week, 'but I haven't decided yet.'\n\n| Luka | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | has moved | moved | was moving | had moved |\n| 2 | said | told | spoke | talked |\n| 3 | been | was | go | visited |\n| 4 | made | has made | makes | had made |\n| 5 | overcome | overtake | overlook | overdo |\n| 6 | recognise | realise | remember | remind |\n| 7 | think | 'm thinking | 've thought | thought |\n\n**Zadanie 9.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą.\n\n> Although Kasia and Ola are identical twins, their personalities could not be more **(1)** ______. Kasia is the life and soul of every party, while Ola prefers to stay at home with a good book. 'People often **(2)** ______ us up,' Ola laughs, 'but only until we start talking.' When they were children, Kasia always **(3)** ______ the decisions, and Ola simply followed her. Things changed when they **(4)** ______ to different universities. 'For the first time in my life I had to stand on my own two **(5)** ______,' Ola explains. Today she is just as independent as her sister – she simply shows it **(6)** ______ quietly.\n\n| Luka | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | similar | different | equal | same |\n| 2 | mix | take | make | put |\n| 3 | did | took | made | had |\n| 4 | went | have gone | were going | had been |\n| 5 | legs | hands | feet | knees |\n| 6 | most | more | much | many |\n\n**Zadanie 10.** Podkreśl właściwą formę czasownika.\n\n1. I *have known / knew* Marta since we were five.\n2. When I arrived, the lesson *already started / had already started*.\n3. He *has lost / lost* his keys yesterday.\n4. *Have you ever met / Did you ever meet* anyone famous?\n5. She was tired because she *worked / had been working* all day.\n6. We *have lived / lived* in Kraków for three years, but then we moved to Rzeszów.\n\n**Zadanie 11.** Uzupełnij zdania czasownikami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. I ________ (not/see) my best friend since September.\n2. By the time we got to the cinema, the film ________ (finish).\n3. My brother ________ (break) his leg when he was skiing last winter.\n4. This is the first time I ________ (try) Indian food.\n5. She ________ (live) in Spain for ten years before she moved to Poland.\n6. ________ you ever ________ (lose) your temper at school?\n\n**Zadanie 12.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, zgodną z treścią tekstu.\n\n> **Born first, born bossy?**\n>\n> Are you the bossy firstborn, the peacemaking middle child or the spoilt youngest? Many people believe that our position in the family shapes our character, and the idea is popular in magazines and online quizzes. Psychologists, however, are far less convinced.\n>\n> In 2015, researchers analysed data from more than 20,000 people in three countries. They compared brothers and sisters from the same families and looked for differences in traits such as confidence, kindness and imagination. The results were surprising: birth order had almost no effect on personality. The only small difference was in intelligence test scores, where firstborns did slightly better on average – but the gap was so small that nobody would notice it in everyday life.\n>\n> So why does the myth survive? One reason is that we remember examples which confirm our beliefs and forget the ones which don't. If your older brother happens to be well organised, you may see it as proof of the theory, while ignoring your equally organised younger cousin. In addition, family stories are simply more memorable than dry statistics.\n\n1. According to the text, the belief that birth order shapes personality\n    - A. is supported by most psychologists.\n    - B. is common but questioned by experts.\n    - C. was invented by magazine journalists.\n    - D. is popular only among young people.\n2. The 2015 study found that\n    - A. firstborns are noticeably more intelligent.\n    - B. younger children are more imaginative.\n    - C. a person's position in the family hardly influences their character.\n    - D. brothers and sisters usually have very different personalities.\n3. The example of the older brother and the younger cousin shows that\n    - A. organised people usually have younger siblings.\n    - B. people tend to notice evidence which supports what they already think.\n    - C. cousins are more similar to each other than siblings.\n    - D. the theory works in some families but not in others.\n\n**Zadanie 13.** Na podstawie tekstu z zadania 12 zdecyduj, które zdania są prawdziwe (P), a które fałszywe (F).\n\n1. The idea that birth order shapes character often appears in magazines. ___\n2. The 2015 study compared people from different families only. ___\n3. Firstborns scored slightly higher in intelligence tests. ___\n4. The author believes that family stories are more reliable than statistics. ___\n\n**Zadanie 14.** Utwórz przymiotniki od podanych wyrazów.\n\n1. friend → ________\n2. care → ________\n3. ambition → ________\n4. rely → ________\n5. help → ________\n6. create → ________\n\n## Część dla szybszych\n\n**Zadanie 15.** Uzupełnij drugie zdanie tak, aby znaczyło to samo co pierwsze. Użyj słowa w nawiasie bez zmiany formy; wpisz od 2 do 5 słów.\n\n1. I last saw Kate three years ago. (**SEEN**) → I ________ three years.\n2. It's the first time I've eaten sushi. (**NEVER**) → I ________ sushi before.\n3. They started going out two years ago. (**BEEN**) → They ________ two years.\n4. When we arrived, the film had already started. (**BEFORE**) → The film started ________.\n5. He hasn't phoned me since May. (**LAST**) → He ________ May.\n\n**Zadanie 16.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. His ________ (PATIENT) with the children surprised everyone.\n2. She is famous for her ________ (GENEROUS).\n3. It was very ________ (CONSIDER) of you to call me.\n4. The boss criticised him for his ________ (RESPONSIBLE) attitude.\n5. Many teenagers feel ________ (SECURE) about their appearance.\n6. His ________ (HONEST) cost him his job.\n\n**Zadanie 17.** Przetłumacz fragmenty w nawiasach na angielski tak, aby zdania były poprawne.\n\n1. My sister really ________ (jest podobna do) our mother – they even laugh the same way.\n2. I ________ (nie mogę znieść) people who are always late.\n3. She ________ (straciła panowanie nad sobą) when she saw the mess in the kitchen.\n4. He ________ (nigdy wcześniej nie był) so nervous before an exam.\n5. We ________ (znamy się od) primary school.\n\n**Zadanie 18.** Uzupełnij luki w tekście. W każdą lukę wpisz jedno słowo.\n\n> My best friend Zosia is one **(1)** ______ the most generous people I know. She would do anything **(2)** ______ her friends. Last month, **(3)** ______ example, she spent a whole weekend helping me **(4)** ______ my chemistry project. She never gets angry, even **(5)** ______ people are rude to her. I really look up **(6)** ______ her.\n\n**Zadanie 19.** Dopasuj idiomy (1–5) do ich znaczeń (a–e).\n\n| Idiom | Znaczenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. a heart of gold | a. practical and realistic |\n| 2. a pain in the neck | b. a very kind person |\n| 3. down to earth | c. a very annoying person |\n| 4. the life and soul of the party | d. an unfriendly person who shows no emotions |\n| 5. a cold fish | e. the most lively, fun person at a party |\n\n**Zadanie 20.** Przeczytaj tekst, z którego usunięto trzy zdania. Wpisz w luki 1–3 litery A–D. Jedno zdanie nie pasuje do żadnej luki.\n\n> **Can you change your personality?**\n>\n> For a long time, psychologists believed that our character is fixed by the age of thirty. **(1)** ______ Recent research suggests that this is not entirely true. People do change, especially when they take on new roles, such as starting a job or becoming parents. **(2)** ______ For example, many people become more organised and calmer as they get older. However, change rarely happens overnight. **(3)** ______ Small, regular steps – like speaking up once in every meeting – seem to work much better than big promises.\n\n- A. In other words, life itself can shape who we are.\n- B. After that, they said, it hardly changes at all.\n- C. Most people who decide to become more outgoing give up after a few weeks.\n- D. That is why twins always have the same personality.\n", "key": "**Zadanie 1:** 1. impatient · 2. dishonest · 3. unreliable · 4. irresponsible · 5. insensitive · 6. immature · 7. disloyal · 8. inconsiderate\n\n**Zadanie 2:** 1. takes after · 2. put up with · 3. brought up · 4. get on · 5. lost his temper · 6. look up to\n\n**Zadanie 3:** 1. generous · 2. stubborn · 3. arrogant · 4. outgoing · 5. reliable · 6. sensible\n\n**Zadanie 4:** 1. sensitive · 2. sensible · 3. embarrassed · 4. boring · 5. shy · 6. wise\n\n**Zadanie 5:** 1. lose · 2. keeps · 3. make · 4. take · 5. keep · 6. make\n\n**Zadanie 6:** 1. bald · 2. curly · 3. well-built · 4. in her twenties · 5. freckles · 6. wrinkles\n\n**Zadanie 7:** 1. of · 2. of · 3. with · 4. in · 5. on · 6. of\n\n**Zadanie 8:** 1. B (*last year* → Past Simple) · 2. B (*tell somebody*) · 3. A (*had never been abroad*) · 4. B (skutek widoczny teraz → Present Perfect) · 5. A (*overcome a fear*) · 6. A (*recognise* = rozpoznać) · 7. B (plan, nad którym się teraz zastanawia)\n\n**Zadanie 9:** 1. B (*could not be more different*) · 2. A (*mix up* = pomylić) · 3. C (*make decisions*) · 4. A · 5. C (*stand on your own two feet*) · 6. B\n\n**Zadanie 10:** 1. have known · 2. had already started · 3. lost · 4. Have you ever met · 5. had been working · 6. lived\n\n**Zadanie 11:** 1. haven't seen · 2. had finished · 3. broke · 4. have tried · 5. had lived · 6. Have … lost\n\n**Zadanie 12:** 1. B · 2. C · 3. B\n\n**Zadanie 13:** 1. P · 2. F · 3. P · 4. F\n\n**Zadanie 14:** 1. friendly · 2. careful · 3. ambitious · 4. reliable · 5. helpful · 6. creative\n\n**Zadanie 15:** 1. haven't seen Kate for · 2. have never eaten · 3. have been going out for · 4. before we arrived · 5. last phoned me in\n\n**Zadanie 16:** 1. patience · 2. generosity · 3. considerate · 4. irresponsible · 5. insecure · 6. dishonesty\n\n**Zadanie 17:** 1. takes after / looks like · 2. can't stand / can't put up with · 3. lost her temper · 4. had never been · 5. have known each other since\n\n**Zadanie 18:** 1. of · 2. for · 3. for · 4. with · 5. when / if · 6. to\n\n**Zadanie 19:** 1b · 2c · 3a · 4e · 5d\n\n**Zadanie 20:** 1. B · 2. A · 3. C (zdanie D nie pasuje)\n\n## Słówka z zestawu\n\n| English | Po polsku |\n| --- | --- |\n| stubborn | uparty |\n| arrogant / big-headed | zarozumiały |\n| reliable | godny zaufania, niezawodny |\n| sensible | rozsądny |\n| sensitive | wrażliwy |\n| outgoing | towarzyski, otwarty |\n| considerate | taktowny, liczy się z innymi |\n| mature | dojrzały |\n| well-built | dobrze zbudowany |\n| freckles | piegi |\n| wrinkles | zmarszczki |\n| take after somebody | być podobnym do kogoś (z rodziny) |\n| put up with | znosić, tolerować |\n| bring up | wychowywać |\n| look up to | podziwiać, wzorować się na |\n| lose one's temper | stracić panowanie nad sobą |\n| overcome a fear | pokonać strach |\n| mix somebody up | pomylić kogoś z kimś |\n| stand on your own two feet | być samodzielnym |\n| fed up with | mieć dość |\n| a heart of gold | złote serce |\n| down to earth | twardo stąpający po ziemi |\n| the life and soul of the party | dusza towarzystwa |\n"}, {"id": "z02", "no": 2, "title": "Zestaw 02: Miejsce zamieszkania · słowotwórstwo", "category": "ang-pr", "topic": "Miejsce zamieszkania · słowotwórstwo · used to · czytanie", "body": "Dział: **Miejsce zamieszkania** (dom, okolica, wynajem) · słowotwórstwo · środki językowe · czytanie. Zadania 1–14 to część podstawowa, zadania 15–20 to część dla szybszych.\n\n## Część podstawowa\n\n**Zadanie 1.** Połącz początki wyrażeń (1–8) z ich zakończeniami (a–h).\n\n| Początek | Zakończenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. a run-down | a. living |\n| 2. the cost of | b. flats |\n| 3. to do up | c. an old house |\n| 4. a detached | d. the rent |\n| 5. a block of | e. area |\n| 6. to pay | f. house |\n| 7. to move | g. a flat with friends |\n| 8. to share | h. out of your parents' house |\n\n**Zadanie 2.** Uzupełnij zdania słowami z ramki w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n> commute · landlord · suburbs · neighbourhood · tenant · deposit\n\n1. We live in the ________ and it takes me an hour to get to the city centre.\n2. The ________ refused to fix the broken heating.\n3. My daily ________ to school takes about forty minutes by bus.\n4. When you rent a flat, you usually have to pay a ________ before you move in.\n5. It's a quiet ________ with lots of parks and friendly people.\n6. The previous ________ left the flat in a terrible state.\n\n**Zadanie 3.** Dopasuj nazwy domów z ramki do definicji.\n\n> detached house · semi-detached house · terraced house · block of flats · cottage · studio flat\n\n1. a small, old house in the countryside → ________\n2. a house which is not joined to any other house → ________\n3. one of a row of houses joined together on both sides → ________\n4. a house joined to one other house on one side → ________\n5. a large building divided into many flats → ________\n6. a flat with one main room for living and sleeping → ________\n\n**Zadanie 4.** Podkreśl właściwe słowo.\n\n1. We keep our old toys in the *attic / ceiling* under the roof.\n2. In Britain, the *ground / first* floor is the one at street level.\n3. A big lamp hangs from the *ceiling / roof* in the living room.\n4. They always park the car on the *driveway / pavement* in front of the house.\n5. My dad keeps his tools in the *basement / loft* under the house.\n6. In summer we have breakfast on the *balcony / corridor*.\n\n**Zadanie 5.** Uzupełnij zdania czasownikami frazowymi z ramki w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n> move in · move out · do up · settle in · clear out · put up\n\n1. We're going to ________ the old house and then sell it for a higher price.\n2. It took the children a few weeks to ________ at their new school.\n3. The new tenants will ________ on Monday.\n4. I need to ________ the garage – it's full of junk.\n5. Can you help me ________ these shelves in my room?\n6. Most young people ________ of their parents' house in their twenties.\n\n**Zadanie 6.** Uzupełnij zdania przymiotnikami z ramki.\n\n> spacious · cramped · cosy · run-down · well-equipped · peaceful\n\n1. The kitchen is very ________ – it has a dishwasher, an oven and a microwave.\n2. Five people living in one small room? That's really ________!\n3. The living room is ________ enough for a big party.\n4. With the fireplace and soft blankets, the room feels really ________.\n5. The building is ________ – the walls are cracked and some windows are broken.\n6. We live in a ________ village where nothing ever happens.\n\n**Zadanie 7.** Uzupełnij zdania właściwymi przyimkami.\n\n1. We live ________ the third floor.\n2. Many families move ________ the suburbs when they have children.\n3. Our house is ________ the end of the street.\n4. They live ________ the outskirts of Kraków.\n5. I grew up ________ the countryside.\n6. The bakery is right next ________ our block.\n\n**Zadanie 8.** Przeczytaj tekst. Uzupełnij luki wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n> Living in a big city has many **(1)** ________ (ADVANTAGE), but for young people the biggest problem is the **(2)** ________ (SHORT) of cheap flats. Rents have risen **(3)** ________ (DRAMATIC) over the last ten years, and many students now share small, **(4)** ________ (FURNISH) rooms with no beds or wardrobes. Some of them say the situation is completely **(5)** ________ (ACCEPT). Local authorities have promised to build more **(6)** ________ (AFFORD) housing, but so far little has changed.\n\n**Zadanie 9.** Utwórz rzeczowniki od podanych czasowników.\n\n1. improve → ________\n2. decorate → ________\n3. furnish → ________\n4. own → ________ (osoba)\n5. renovate → ________\n6. accommodate → ________\n\n**Zadanie 10.** Utwórz przeczenia podanych przymiotników.\n\n1. comfortable → ________\n2. convenient → ________\n3. affordable → ________\n4. safe → ________\n5. legal → ________\n6. practical → ________\n\n**Zadanie 11.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, tak aby otrzymać logiczny i gramatycznie poprawny tekst.\n\n> When Tom moved to Manchester, he had to find a flat **(1)** ______ quickly. He looked at dozens of adverts, but most flats were either too expensive or in a **(2)** ______ condition. Finally, he found a small studio in a quiet area. The landlord asked him to pay a **(3)** ______ of one month's rent and to sign a **(4)** ______ for a year. The flat was **(5)** ______ furnished, so Tom only had to buy a bed. 'It's not perfect,' he says, 'but I've finally got a place I can **(6)** ______ home.'\n\n| Luka | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | much | too | very | so |\n| 2 | terrific | terrible | tender | tremendous |\n| 3 | debt | fine | fee | deposit |\n| 4 | contact | contract | content | context |\n| 5 | part | partly | party | parting |\n| 6 | tell | say | call | name |\n\n**Zadanie 12.** Przeczytaj tekst, z którego usunięto trzy zdania. Wpisz w luki 1–3 litery A–D. Jedno zdanie nie pasuje do żadnej luki.\n\n> **Small house, big change?**\n>\n> Over the past decade, tiny houses – homes smaller than 40 square metres – have become a symbol of a simpler lifestyle. **(1)** ________ Many owners say that moving into such a small space forced them to get rid of things they never really needed.\n>\n> Supporters also point to the financial benefits. A tiny house costs a fraction of the price of a traditional home, and bills for heating and electricity are much lower. **(2)** ________ For young people who cannot afford a mortgage, this can be the only realistic way of owning a home.\n>\n> However, life in a tiny house is not for everyone. Finding a legal place to put one can be difficult, as many towns do not allow people to live permanently in buildings of this size. **(3)** ________ As one owner admitted, sharing 20 square metres with a partner and a dog requires a lot of patience.\n\n- A. As a result, some owners can pay for their house in just a few years.\n- B. Living together in such a small space can also put pressure on relationships.\n- C. They are especially popular among people who want to own fewer possessions.\n- D. That is why most tiny houses are built by professional companies.\n\n**Zadanie 13.** Na podstawie tekstu z zadania 12 zdecyduj, które zdania są prawdziwe (P), a które fałszywe (F).\n\n1. Tiny houses are bigger than 40 square metres. ___\n2. Some owners say they now own fewer unnecessary things. ___\n3. Tiny houses are cheaper to heat than traditional homes. ___\n4. It is easy to find a legal place to put a tiny house. ___\n\n**Zadanie 14.** Uzupełnij zdania konstrukcją **used to**, **be used to** albo **get used to** i czasownikiem w nawiasie.\n\n1. I ________ (live) in a flat, but now we have a house.\n2. It took me months to ________ (wake up) so early for school.\n3. She's from Norway, so she ________ (live) in a cold climate.\n4. ________ you ________ (share) a room with your brother when you were little?\n5. I'm not ________ (sleep) in such a noisy area.\n6. We ________ (not / have) a garden before we moved.\n\n## Część dla szybszych\n\n**Zadanie 15.** Przetłumacz fragmenty w nawiasach na angielski tak, aby zdania były poprawne. Pilnuj czasu i szyku całego zdania.\n\n1. If I had known about the noise, I ________ (nie wynająłbym tego mieszkania).\n2. We ________ (właśnie remontujemy kuchnię), so we're eating out this week.\n3. I'd rather ________ (mieszkać na przedmieściach) than in the city centre.\n4. Our flat ________ (jest w tej chwili odnawiane), so we're staying with my aunt.\n5. It's high time we ________ (wyprowadzili się od rodziców).\n\n**Zadanie 16.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. The flat was spacious, but the ________ (NEIGHBOUR) was rather dangerous at night.\n2. There is a serious ________ (SHORT) of student accommodation in our city.\n3. They decided to ________ (LARGE) the kitchen by knocking down a wall.\n4. The building was ________ (SUIT) for families with small children – it had no lift.\n5. The ________ (OWN) of the house lives abroad and rarely visits.\n6. Rents in the city centre are ________ (REASON) high, even for people with good jobs.\n\n**Zadanie 17.** Uzupełnij drugie zdanie tak, aby znaczyło to samo co pierwsze. Użyj słowa w nawiasie bez zmiany formy; wpisz od 2 do 5 słów.\n\n1. It's two years since we moved here. (**LIVED**) → We ________ two years.\n2. Someone is painting our kitchen at the moment. (**HAVING**) → We ________ at the moment.\n3. I'd prefer to live in the countryside. (**RATHER**) → I ________ in the countryside.\n4. The flat was too expensive for us to rent. (**ENOUGH**) → The flat wasn't ________ to rent.\n5. I have never shared a room before, so it feels strange. (**USED**) → I'm not ________ a room.\n\n**Zadanie 18.** Uzupełnij luki w tekście. W każdą lukę wpisz jedno słowo.\n\n> When my family moved **(1)** ______ the city to a small village, I hated it at first. There was nothing to do and the nearest shop was miles **(2)** ______. However, I soon got used **(3)** ______ the peace and quiet. Now I can't imagine living anywhere **(4)** ______. Our house is much bigger **(5)** ______ our old flat, and I finally have a room of my **(6)** ______.\n\n**Zadanie 19.** Dopasuj wyrażenia (1–5) do ich znaczeń (a–e).\n\n| Wyrażenie | Znaczenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. make yourself at home | a. to rise very quickly (about prices) |\n| 2. get on like a house on fire | b. to become very angry |\n| 3. hit the roof | c. relax and behave as if this were your house |\n| 4. go through the roof | d. to like each other a lot from the start |\n| 5. feel at home | e. to feel comfortable and relaxed in a place |\n\n**Zadanie 20.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, zgodną z treścią tekstu.\n\n> **Flatmates: friends or enemies?**\n>\n> Sharing a flat with other people can be a great way to save money, but it is not always easy. According to a recent survey of 2,000 students, the most common cause of arguments is not noise or money but cleaning. Almost half of those asked said they had argued with a flatmate about dirty dishes. Experts suggest agreeing on a cleaning rota before moving in, as it is much harder to change habits once problems appear. Interestingly, students who shared with friends were not happier than those who lived with strangers – in fact, some said that a close friendship had ended because of everyday conflicts at home.\n\n1. According to the survey, students most often argue about\n    - A. noise.\n    - B. rent.\n    - C. housework.\n    - D. guests.\n2. Experts recommend\n    - A. choosing flatmates very carefully.\n    - B. agreeing on rules early.\n    - C. living alone if possible.\n    - D. never sharing a flat with friends.\n3. The last sentence suggests that\n    - A. friends make the best flatmates.\n    - B. living together can damage a friendship.\n    - C. strangers are usually untidy.\n    - D. most students prefer living alone.\n", "key": "**Zadanie 1:** 1e · 2a · 3c · 4f · 5b · 6d · 7h · 8g\n\n**Zadanie 2:** 1. suburbs · 2. landlord · 3. commute · 4. deposit · 5. neighbourhood · 6. tenant\n\n**Zadanie 3:** 1. cottage · 2. detached house · 3. terraced house · 4. semi-detached house · 5. block of flats · 6. studio flat\n\n**Zadanie 4:** 1. attic · 2. ground · 3. ceiling · 4. driveway · 5. basement · 6. balcony\n\n**Zadanie 5:** 1. do up · 2. settle in · 3. move in · 4. clear out · 5. put up · 6. move out\n\n**Zadanie 6:** 1. well-equipped · 2. cramped · 3. spacious · 4. cosy · 5. run-down · 6. peaceful\n\n**Zadanie 7:** 1. on · 2. to · 3. at · 4. on · 5. in · 6. to\n\n**Zadanie 8:** 1. advantages · 2. shortage · 3. dramatically · 4. unfurnished · 5. unacceptable · 6. affordable\n\n**Zadanie 9:** 1. improvement · 2. decoration · 3. furniture · 4. owner · 5. renovation · 6. accommodation\n\n**Zadanie 10:** 1. uncomfortable · 2. inconvenient · 3. unaffordable · 4. unsafe · 5. illegal · 6. impractical\n\n**Zadanie 11:** 1. C (*very quickly*) · 2. B (*in a terrible condition*) · 3. D (*deposit* = kaucja) · 4. B (*contract* = umowa) · 5. B (*partly furnished*) · 6. C (*call somewhere home*)\n\n**Zadanie 12:** 1. C · 2. A · 3. B (zdanie D nie pasuje)\n\n**Zadanie 13:** 1. F · 2. P · 3. P · 4. F\n\n**Zadanie 14:** 1. used to live · 2. get used to waking up · 3. is used to living · 4. Did … use to share · 5. used to sleeping · 6. didn't use to have\n\n**Zadanie 15:** 1. wouldn't have rented this flat · 2. are (just) doing up the kitchen / are renovating the kitchen · 3. live in the suburbs · 4. is being renovated / is being done up at the moment · 5. moved out of our parents' house\n\n**Zadanie 16:** 1. neighbourhood · 2. shortage · 3. enlarge · 4. unsuitable · 5. owner · 6. unreasonably\n\n**Zadanie 17:** 1. have lived here for · 2. are having our kitchen painted · 3. would rather live · 4. cheap enough for us · 5. used to sharing\n\n**Zadanie 18:** 1. from · 2. away · 3. to · 4. else · 5. than · 6. own\n\n**Zadanie 19:** 1c · 2d · 3b · 4a · 5e\n\n**Zadanie 20:** 1. C · 2. B · 3. B\n\n## Słówka z zestawu\n\n| English | Po polsku |\n| --- | --- |\n| run-down area | zaniedbana okolica |\n| cost of living | koszty utrzymania |\n| do up | wyremontować, odnowić |\n| detached / semi-detached / terraced house | dom wolnostojący / bliźniak / szeregowiec |\n| landlord / tenant | właściciel mieszkania / najemca |\n| deposit | kaucja |\n| commute | dojeżdżać (do pracy, szkoły) |\n| suburbs / outskirts | przedmieścia / obrzeża |\n| attic / basement | strych / piwnica |\n| ceiling | sufit |\n| driveway | podjazd |\n| settle in | zaaklimatyzować się |\n| clear out | wysprzątać, opróżnić |\n| spacious / cramped | przestronny / ciasny |\n| well-equipped | dobrze wyposażony |\n| shortage | niedobór, brak |\n| affordable | przystępny cenowo |\n| (un)furnished | (nie)umeblowany |\n| accommodation | zakwaterowanie |\n| mortgage | kredyt hipoteczny |\n| hit the roof | wpaść w szał |\n| go through the roof | gwałtownie wzrosnąć (o cenach) |\n| get on like a house on fire | świetnie się dogadywać |\n"}, {"id": "z03", "no": 3, "title": "Zestaw 03: Edukacja · parafrazy", "category": "ang-pr", "topic": "Edukacja · parafrazy · gerund i bezokolicznik · czytanie", "body": "Dział: **Edukacja** (szkoła, przedmioty, egzaminy, studia) · parafrazy · środki językowe · czytanie. Zadania 1–14 to część podstawowa, zadania 15–20 to część dla szybszych.\n\n## Część podstawowa\n\n**Zadanie 1.** Uzupełnij kolokacje czasownikami z ramki w odpowiedniej formie. Każdy czasownik wykorzystaj raz.\n\n> sit · pass · fail · take · revise · cheat · skip · hand in\n\n1. If you don't study, you will ________ the exam.\n2. I need to ________ for my history test – it's tomorrow!\n3. Students who ________ in exams are disqualified.\n4. She ________ all her exams with flying colours.\n5. We have to ________ our essays by Friday.\n6. He often ________ PE lessons and goes to the café instead.\n7. Most students ________ the matura exam in May.\n8. I'd like to ________ notes during the lecture.\n\n**Zadanie 2.** Uzupełnij zdania słowami z ramki.\n\n> curriculum · tuition fees · scholarship · degree · timetable · boarding school\n\n1. She got a ________ because her grades were excellent, so she doesn't pay for her studies.\n2. In Britain, many universities charge high ________.\n3. He has a ________ in economics from the University of Warsaw.\n4. According to our ________, we have maths every day.\n5. The national ________ decides what students must learn in each subject.\n6. At a ________, students live at school during the term.\n\n**Zadanie 3.** Dopasuj wyrażenia (1–6) do znaczeń (a–f).\n\n| Wyrażenie | Znaczenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. play truant | a. study a lot in a short time before an exam |\n| 2. learn by heart | b. stay away from school without permission |\n| 3. cram | c. memorise something exactly |\n| 4. catch up on | d. leave school or university before finishing |\n| 5. drop out | e. do work you didn't do earlier |\n| 6. fall behind | f. make less progress than others |\n\n**Zadanie 4.** Podkreśl właściwe słowo.\n\n1. Our teacher *learnt / taught* us how to write a formal letter.\n2. I'm going to *make / take* an exam in English next month.\n3. She got a good *note / mark* in her chemistry test.\n4. He is studying to become a *lecturer / reader* at the university.\n5. Please *raise / rise* your hand if you know the answer.\n6. The *headteacher / principle* called my parents to school.\n\n**Zadanie 5.** Uzupełnij zdania właściwymi przyimkami.\n\n1. She is very good ________ maths.\n2. I'm worried ________ my exam results.\n3. We had to write an essay ________ climate change.\n4. He's studying ________ a degree in law.\n5. They were punished ________ cheating in the test.\n6. Students should concentrate ________ their weakest subjects.\n\n**Zadanie 6.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. All the ________ (PARTICIPATE) in the course received a certificate.\n2. Your ________ (ATTEND) at classes is compulsory.\n3. She has an ________ (IMPRESS) knowledge of history.\n4. Learning a language takes time and ________ (PATIENT).\n5. The test was ________ (SURPRISE) easy.\n6. He was ________ (EXPEL) from school for bad behaviour.\n\n**Zadanie 7.** Parafrazy – poziom 1. Uzupełnij drugie zdanie tak, aby znaczyło to samo co pierwsze. Użyj słowa w nawiasie bez zmiany formy; wpisz od 2 do 5 słów.\n\n1. I started learning French three years ago. (**FOR**) → I have ________ three years.\n2. \"Do your homework,\" the teacher told us. (**TOLD**) → The teacher ________ our homework.\n3. It isn't necessary for you to bring a dictionary. (**HAVE**) → You ________ bring a dictionary.\n4. I regret not studying harder. (**WISH**) → I ________ harder.\n5. The exam was so difficult that nobody passed it. (**SUCH**) → It was ________ that nobody passed it.\n\n**Zadanie 8.** Parafrazy – poziom 2. Postępuj tak samo jak w zadaniu 7.\n\n1. They will announce the results next week. (**BE**) → The results ________ next week.\n2. Maybe she forgot about the test. (**HAVE**) → She ________ about the test.\n3. I prefer studying alone to studying in a group. (**RATHER**) → I ________ than in a group.\n4. If you don't revise, you won't pass. (**UNLESS**) → You won't pass ________.\n5. It's a pity I can't go to that university. (**ONLY**) → If ________ to that university.\n\n**Zadanie 9.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, tak aby otrzymać logiczny i gramatycznie poprawny tekst.\n\n> When I was in my last year at secondary school, I was terrified of the final exams. My friends spent hours **(1)** ______ for them every evening, but I simply couldn't concentrate. My parents suggested **(2)** ______ a tutor. At first I refused, but after I **(3)** ______ my mock exam in maths, I changed my mind. My tutor showed me how to **(4)** ______ my time effectively and how to deal with stress. In the end, I passed all my exams and got a **(5)** ______ at the university I had dreamed of. Looking back, I wish I **(6)** ______ for help earlier.\n\n| Luka | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | revising | revise | to revise | revised |\n| 2 | to find | finding | find | found |\n| 3 | had failed | have failed | was failing | fail |\n| 4 | spend | manage | take | lead |\n| 5 | seat | chair | place | position |\n| 6 | asked | have asked | had asked | would ask |\n\n**Zadanie 10.** Uzupełnij zdania czasownikami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie (gerund lub bezokolicznik).\n\n1. I can't afford ________ (fail) this exam.\n2. She avoided ________ (answer) the teacher's question.\n3. We decided ________ (study) together in the library.\n4. He admitted ________ (copy) his friend's homework.\n5. Don't forget ________ (bring) your calculator tomorrow.\n6. I remember ________ (be) very nervous on my first day at school.\n\n**Zadanie 11.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, zgodną z treścią tekstu.\n\n> **Homework: help or harm?**\n>\n> For many teenagers, homework is the least favourite part of school. Some education experts agree with them. They argue that long hours of homework leave students tired and stressed, with no time for sport, hobbies or family. In Finland, a country famous for its excellent schools, pupils get very little homework, yet they regularly achieve top results in international tests.\n>\n> However, other researchers point out that the situation is more complicated. Studies suggest that a moderate amount of homework – around one hour a day for secondary school students – can improve results, especially in subjects like maths and languages, where regular practice is important. The problem begins when homework becomes too long or too difficult. Then students either give up or copy answers from the internet, and the work brings no benefit at all.\n>\n> The key, it seems, is not whether to give homework, but what kind. Short tasks that students can do on their own are far more effective than long projects that require help from parents.\n\n1. The writer mentions Finland to show that\n    - A. Finnish students do more homework than others.\n    - B. good results are possible without much homework.\n    - C. homework is popular in Scandinavian schools.\n    - D. international tests are not reliable.\n2. According to the second paragraph, homework\n    - A. is useless in all subjects.\n    - B. should take at least three hours a day.\n    - C. can help when there is not too much of it.\n    - D. is especially important in sport and art.\n3. The main idea of the last paragraph is that\n    - A. parents should help their children more.\n    - B. long projects are the best kind of homework.\n    - C. the type of homework matters most.\n    - D. homework should be banned.\n\n**Zadanie 12.** Na podstawie tekstu z zadania 11 zdecyduj, które zdania są prawdziwe (P), a które fałszywe (F).\n\n1. Some experts believe homework causes stress. ___\n2. Finnish pupils get a lot of homework. ___\n3. Students sometimes copy answers when homework is too hard. ___\n4. The author thinks homework should always be long and difficult. ___\n\n**Zadanie 13.** Uzupełnij luki w tekście. W każdą lukę wpisz jedno słowo.\n\n> My brother has always been interested **(1)** ______ science. When he was ten, he spent most of **(2)** ______ free time doing experiments in the kitchen. Now he is studying chemistry **(3)** ______ university. He says the course is much harder **(4)** ______ he expected, but he enjoys it a lot. He is hoping to get a job in a laboratory **(5)** ______ he graduates. I'm sure he will succeed, because he never gives **(6)** ______.\n\n**Zadanie 14.** Dobierz do każdej sytuacji (1–4) właściwą reakcję (A–E). Jedna reakcja nie pasuje.\n\n1. Your friend says he failed his driving test again.\n2. A classmate asks if she can borrow your notes.\n3. Your teacher asks why you didn't hand in your essay.\n4. Your friend got the best mark in the class.\n\n- A. Sure, but please give them back tomorrow.\n- B. I'm really sorry, I completely forgot about it.\n- C. Congratulations! You really deserve it.\n- D. Don't worry, third time lucky!\n- E. I'm afraid I can't stand exams.\n\n## Część dla szybszych\n\n**Zadanie 15.** Parafrazy – poziom 3. Postępuj tak samo jak w zadaniu 7.\n\n1. They made us wear uniforms at school. (**WERE**) → We ________ uniforms at school.\n2. I had never seen such a strict teacher before. (**STRICTEST**) → She was ________ ever seen.\n3. As soon as he arrived, the exam started. (**SOONER**) → No ________ than the exam started.\n4. It was wrong of you to copy the answers. (**SHOULDN'T**) → You ________ the answers.\n5. People say that this school is the best in the city. (**SAID**) → This school ________ the best in the city.\n\n**Zadanie 16.** Przetłumacz fragmenty w nawiasach na angielski tak, aby zdania były poprawne.\n\n1. ________ (Gdybym wiedział o teście), I would have studied.\n2. She ________ (zdaje maturę) next year.\n3. The teacher made ________ (nas przepisać) the whole essay.\n4. ________ (Nie tylko zdał egzamin), but he also got the highest score.\n5. I'm not used ________ (do wstawania) so early.\n\n**Zadanie 17.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. Many students find the ________ (TRANSIT) from school to university difficult.\n2. Teachers should encourage ________ (CREATE) in their students.\n3. The course is ________ (RECOMMEND) for beginners.\n4. His ________ (KNOW) of grammar is excellent.\n5. She is one of the most ________ (SUCCESS) students in the school.\n6. ________ (FORTUNATE), I missed the bus and was late for the exam.\n\n**Zadanie 18.** Wybierz właściwą odpowiedź.\n\n1. The school has decided to ________ mobile phones during lessons.\n    - A. forbid\n    - B. ban\n    - C. refuse\n    - D. deny\n2. You'd better ________ your notes before the test.\n    - A. go over\n    - B. go off\n    - C. go by\n    - D. go out\n3. Students are ________ to wear a uniform.\n    - A. required\n    - B. obliged to\n    - C. needed\n    - D. demanded\n4. He got ________ marks in all his exams.\n    - A. top\n    - B. peak\n    - C. tip\n    - D. upper\n\n**Zadanie 19.** Dopasuj wyrażenia (1–5) do znaczeń (a–e).\n\n| Wyrażenie | Znaczenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. pass with flying colours | a. learn by watching and doing, not from books |\n| 2. learn the ropes | b. be the teacher's favourite student |\n| 3. teacher's pet | c. pass very successfully |\n| 4. hit the books | d. learn the basic rules of a new job or activity |\n| 5. learn something the hard way | e. start studying seriously |\n| | f. learn from your own unpleasant experience |\n\nUwaga: jedno znaczenie zostaje.\n\n**Zadanie 20.** Przeczytaj tekst, z którego usunięto trzy zdania. Wpisz w luki 1–3 litery A–D. Jedno zdanie nie pasuje do żadnej luki.\n\n> **Learning online**\n>\n> When schools closed during the pandemic, millions of students had to learn online almost overnight. **(1)** ______ Some students enjoyed the freedom of planning their own day, while others felt lonely and found it hard to stay motivated.\n>\n> Teachers also had to change the way they worked. **(2)** ______ Many of them discovered useful tools, such as online quizzes and shared documents, which they still use today.\n>\n> Today, most students are back in classrooms, but online learning has not disappeared. **(3)** ______ For example, students who are ill can now follow lessons from home instead of missing them completely.\n\n- A. It is now used to support traditional lessons rather than replace them.\n- B. For many of them, it was a completely new experience.\n- C. Preparing a good online lesson often took twice as long as a normal one.\n- D. That is why online schools are now more popular than traditional ones.\n", "key": "**Zadanie 1:** 1. fail · 2. revise · 3. cheat · 4. passed · 5. hand in · 6. skips · 7. sit · 8. take\n\n**Zadanie 2:** 1. scholarship · 2. tuition fees · 3. degree · 4. timetable · 5. curriculum · 6. boarding school\n\n**Zadanie 3:** 1b · 2c · 3a · 4e · 5d · 6f\n\n**Zadanie 4:** 1. taught · 2. take · 3. mark · 4. lecturer · 5. raise · 6. headteacher\n\n**Zadanie 5:** 1. at · 2. about · 3. on / about · 4. for · 5. for · 6. on\n\n**Zadanie 6:** 1. participants · 2. attendance · 3. impressive · 4. patience · 5. surprisingly · 6. expelled\n\n**Zadanie 7:** 1. been learning French for / learnt French for · 2. told us to do · 3. don't have to · 4. wish I had studied · 5. such a difficult exam\n\n**Zadanie 8:** 1. will be announced · 2. may / might / must have forgotten · 3. would rather study alone · 4. unless you revise · 5. only I could go\n\n**Zadanie 9:** 1. A (*spend time doing*) · 2. B (*suggest doing*) · 3. A (wcześniej niż zmiana decyzji → Past Perfect) · 4. B (*manage your time*) · 5. C (*get a place at university*) · 6. C (*wish* + Past Perfect o przeszłości)\n\n**Zadanie 10:** 1. to fail · 2. answering · 3. to study · 4. copying · 5. to bring · 6. being\n\n**Zadanie 11:** 1. B · 2. C · 3. C\n\n**Zadanie 12:** 1. P · 2. F · 3. P · 4. F\n\n**Zadanie 13:** 1. in · 2. his · 3. at · 4. than · 5. when / after / once · 6. up\n\n**Zadanie 14:** 1. D · 2. A · 3. B · 4. C (reakcja E nie pasuje)\n\n**Zadanie 15:** 1. were made to wear · 2. the strictest teacher I had · 3. sooner had he arrived · 4. shouldn't have copied · 5. is said to be\n\n**Zadanie 16:** 1. If I had known about the test · 2. is taking / is sitting her matura (exam) · 3. us rewrite · 4. Not only did he pass the exam · 5. to getting up\n\n**Zadanie 17:** 1. transition · 2. creativity · 3. recommended · 4. knowledge · 5. successful · 6. Unfortunately\n\n**Zadanie 18:** 1. B · 2. A · 3. A · 4. A\n\n**Zadanie 19:** 1c · 2d · 3b · 4e · 5f (znaczenie a zostaje)\n\n**Zadanie 20:** 1. B · 2. C · 3. A (zdanie D nie pasuje)\n\n## Słówka z zestawu\n\n| English | Po polsku |\n| --- | --- |\n| sit / take an exam | przystąpić do egzaminu |\n| revise for | powtarzać do (egzaminu) |\n| hand in | oddać (pracę) |\n| skip a lesson / play truant | opuścić lekcję / wagarować |\n| tuition fees | czesne |\n| scholarship | stypendium |\n| degree | dyplom, stopień naukowy |\n| curriculum | program nauczania |\n| boarding school | szkoła z internatem |\n| cram | wkuwać |\n| learn by heart | nauczyć się na pamięć |\n| catch up on | nadrobić |\n| drop out | rzucić szkołę / studia |\n| fall behind | zostać w tyle |\n| attendance | frekwencja, obecność |\n| expel | wyrzucić (ze szkoły) |\n| lecturer | wykładowca |\n| mock exam | próbna matura / próbny egzamin |\n| go over | przejrzeć, powtórzyć |\n| pass with flying colours | zdać śpiewająco |\n| learn the ropes | wdrożyć się, poznać zasady |\n| hit the books | zabrać się do nauki |\n"}, {"id": "z04", "no": 4, "title": "Zestaw 04: Praca · tłumaczenia fragmentów zdań", "category": "ang-pr", "topic": "Praca · tłumaczenia · strona bierna · czytanie", "body": "Dział: **Praca** (zawody, rekrutacja, warunki pracy) · tłumaczenia fragmentów zdań · środki językowe · czytanie. Zadania 1–14 to część podstawowa, zadania 15–20 to część dla szybszych.\n\n## Część podstawowa\n\n**Zadanie 1.** Uzupełnij zdania słowami z ramki.\n\n> salary · wage · employer · employee · applicant · overtime · shift · pension\n\n1. Nurses often work the night ________ in hospitals.\n2. She earns a good ________ – over 8,000 złoty a month.\n3. Workers in the factory are paid a weekly ________.\n4. Every ________ has to fill in a form and send a CV.\n5. My ________ has offered me a promotion.\n6. Each ________ gets 26 days of paid holiday a year.\n7. When he retires, he will receive a ________.\n8. I had to work ________ last week because we had a deadline.\n\n**Zadanie 2.** Uzupełnij kolokacje czasownikami z ramki w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n> apply · run · earn · get · lose · go · set up · take on\n\n1. He ________ his job when the company closed.\n2. My aunt ________ her own business ten years ago.\n3. She has ________ for a job in a bank.\n4. Our boss ________ a small restaurant in the city centre.\n5. The company is going to ________ twenty new workers.\n6. He ________ a living as a taxi driver.\n7. I hope to ________ a promotion next year.\n8. The workers ________ on strike for higher wages.\n\n**Zadanie 3.** Dopasuj nazwy zawodów z ramki do opisów.\n\n> plumber · surgeon · accountant · lawyer · receptionist · estate agent\n\n1. someone who helps people to buy or sell houses → ________\n2. someone who repairs pipes and water systems → ________\n3. a doctor who performs operations → ________\n4. someone who keeps or checks financial records → ________\n5. someone who answers the phone and welcomes visitors in a hotel or office → ________\n6. someone who gives legal advice → ________\n\n**Zadanie 4.** Podkreśl właściwe słowo.\n\n1. I'd like to *work / job* as a translator.\n2. She was *sacked / resigned* for stealing money from the company.\n3. He decided to *retire / resign* because he had found a better job.\n4. The *staff / stuff* in this office are very friendly.\n5. We need someone with *experience / experiment* in marketing.\n6. She works *part-time / half-time*, only in the mornings.\n\n**Zadanie 5.** Uzupełnij zdania właściwymi przyimkami.\n\n1. She is responsible ________ training new staff.\n2. He has been ________ the dole for six months.\n3. I'm applying ________ a position in a law firm.\n4. My dad works ________ a big international company.\n5. He's ________ charge of the sales department.\n6. She's very experienced ________ customer service.\n\n**Zadanie 6.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. The level of ________ (EMPLOY) in the region is very high – many people can't find work.\n2. She sent her ________ (APPLY) form yesterday.\n3. He is a ________ (PROFESSION) photographer.\n4. The new manager is very ________ (DEMAND) – she expects a lot from us.\n5. The job is well paid but ________ (STRESS).\n6. In Poland, many people go into ________ (RETIRE) at the age of 65.\n\n**Zadanie 7.** Tłumaczenia – poziom 1. Przetłumacz fragmenty w nawiasach na angielski tak, aby zdania były poprawne.\n\n1. I ________ (szukam pracy) for two months now.\n2. She ________ (została zatrudniona) as a manager last year.\n3. If I ________ (dostanę tę pracę), I will move to Kraków.\n4. He ________ (pracuje tu od) 2020.\n5. ________ (Jak długo pracujesz) in this company?\n\n**Zadanie 8.** Tłumaczenia – poziom 2.\n\n1. I'd like ________ (żebyś przeczytał) my CV before I send it.\n2. She ________ (nie musiała pracować) at the weekend.\n3. My boss ________ (kazał mi zostać) after work.\n4. ________ (Gdyby nie była tak zmęczona), she would accept the offer.\n5. He is ________ (najbardziej pracowitą osobą) I know.\n\n**Zadanie 9.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, tak aby otrzymać logiczny i gramatycznie poprawny tekst.\n\n> Last summer I got my first **(1)** ______ job in a café. At first I thought it would be easy, but I soon realised how hard it was. I had to be at work at six in the morning, and my **(2)** ______ often lasted ten hours. The pay was not very good, but customers often left **(3)** ______. The manager was strict, but fair, and she taught me how to **(4)** ______ with difficult customers. By the end of the summer I had **(5)** ______ enough money to buy a new laptop. More importantly, I learnt that every job **(6)** ______ respect.\n\n| Luka | A | B | C | D |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | summer | holiday's | summers | holidays |\n| 2 | turn | shift | change | period |\n| 3 | tips | gifts | fees | rewards |\n| 4 | manage | handle | deal | cope |\n| 5 | earned | won | gained | paid |\n| 6 | worth | deserves | needs | merits to |\n\n**Zadanie 10.** Wybierz poprawną formę: strona czynna czy bierna. Uzupełnij zdania czasownikami w nawiasach.\n\n1. The new office ________ (open) next month.\n2. Twenty people ________ (lay off) last week.\n3. Our manager ________ (interview) three candidates yesterday.\n4. All applications must ________ (send) by Friday.\n5. The company ________ (found) in 1995.\n6. Our salaries ________ (raise) recently.\n\n**Zadanie 11.** Przeczytaj tekst. Z podanych odpowiedzi wybierz właściwą, zgodną z treścią tekstu.\n\n> **The four-day week**\n>\n> In recent years, several companies around the world have tested a four-day working week. Employees work 32 hours instead of 40, but their pay stays the same. The results of these experiments have surprised many managers. In a large British trial, most companies reported that productivity stayed the same or even improved. Workers said they felt less tired and took fewer days off sick.\n>\n> Not everyone is convinced, however. Critics point out that the model works well in offices, but it is much harder to introduce in hospitals, schools or shops, which need to stay open five or more days a week. Others worry that employees may feel pressure to do five days' work in four, which could actually increase stress.\n>\n> Despite these doubts, most of the companies that took part in the British trial decided to keep the four-day week after it ended.\n\n1. During the British trial, most companies found that\n    - A. workers produced much less.\n    - B. productivity did not fall.\n    - C. salaries had to be reduced.\n    - D. employees worked longer hours.\n2. According to critics, the four-day week\n    - A. is impossible in every type of job.\n    - B. is easier to introduce in offices than in hospitals.\n    - C. makes employees lazy.\n    - D. costs companies too much money.\n3. The last paragraph suggests that the trial was\n    - A. generally successful.\n    - B. a complete failure.\n    - C. stopped too early.\n    - D. repeated the following year.\n\n**Zadanie 12.** Na podstawie tekstu z zadania 11 zdecyduj, które zdania są prawdziwe (P), a które fałszywe (F).\n\n1. In the four-day week, workers earn less money. ___\n2. Workers in the trial were ill less often. ___\n3. Some people fear the model could increase stress. ___\n4. Most companies returned to the five-day week after the trial. ___\n\n**Zadanie 13.** Uzupełnij luki w tekście. W każdą lukę wpisz jedno słowo.\n\n> Finding a good job is not easy, especially **(1)** ______ you have no experience. That's **(2)** ______ many students do internships during their studies. An internship gives you the chance to find **(3)** ______ what a job is really like. It also looks good **(4)** ______ your CV. Some interns are paid, but others work **(5)** ______ free. Even so, many of them say it was worth it, because they were offered a job **(6)** ______ the end.\n\n**Zadanie 14.** Dobierz do każdej sytuacji (1–4) właściwą reakcję (A–E). Jedna reakcja nie pasuje.\n\n1. At a job interview you are asked about your weaknesses.\n2. Your friend tells you she has just been promoted.\n3. A customer complains that his order is late.\n4. Your boss asks if you can stay longer today.\n\n- A. I'm terribly sorry, I'll check what happened right away.\n- B. I sometimes spend too much time on details, but I'm working on it.\n- C. Well done! You've worked so hard for it.\n- D. I'm afraid I can't today, but I could stay tomorrow.\n- E. I've been working here for two years.\n\n## Część dla szybszych\n\n**Zadanie 15.** Tłumaczenia – poziom 3.\n\n1. ________ (Gdybym przyjął tę ofertę), I would be living in London now.\n2. She ________ (mogła zostać zwolniona) if she had been late again.\n3. ________ (Nigdy wcześniej nie widziałem) such a well-organised office.\n4. I'm looking forward ________ (do rozpoczęcia nowej pracy).\n5. He ________ (podobno zarabia) a fortune.\n\n**Zadanie 16.** Uzupełnij drugie zdanie tak, aby znaczyło to samo co pierwsze. Użyj słowa w nawiasie bez zmiany formy; wpisz od 2 do 5 słów.\n\n1. Someone is going to repair my computer tomorrow. (**HAVE**) → I'm ________ tomorrow.\n2. \"I didn't take the money,\" the cashier said. (**DENIED**) → The cashier ________ the money.\n3. It's possible that the manager forgot about the meeting. (**MAY**) → The manager ________ about the meeting.\n4. She started working here five years ago. (**BEEN**) → She ________ for five years.\n5. You don't need to wear a suit at work. (**NECESSARY**) → It ________ to wear a suit at work.\n\n**Zadanie 17.** Uzupełnij zdania wyrazami w nawiasach w odpowiedniej formie.\n\n1. Her ________ (RESIGN) came as a surprise to everyone.\n2. The workers are fighting for better working ________ (CONDITION).\n3. He was ________ (QUALIFY) for the job – he had no degree.\n4. The company offers good ________ (PROMOTE) opportunities.\n5. She is ________ (EXTREME) good at negotiating.\n6. The ________ (PAY) of bonuses was delayed again.\n\n**Zadanie 18.** Wybierz właściwą odpowiedź.\n\n1. He was ________ because the company didn't need so many workers.\n    - A. made redundant\n    - B. made unemployed\n    - C. done redundant\n    - D. become redundant\n2. I'm afraid I'm ________ my depth in this job – it's too difficult for me.\n    - A. under\n    - B. out of\n    - C. beyond\n    - D. over\n3. She's been working round the ________ to finish the project.\n    - A. watch\n    - B. hour\n    - C. clock\n    - D. time\n4. It's a dead-________ job with no chance of promotion.\n    - A. end\n    - B. stop\n    - C. line\n    - D. point\n\n**Zadanie 19.** Dopasuj wyrażenia (1–5) do znaczeń (a–e).\n\n| Wyrażenie | Znaczenie |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. a nine-to-five job | a. to be given more responsibility or a higher position |\n| 2. climb the career ladder | b. to work very hard |\n| 3. work your fingers to the bone | c. a regular office job with fixed hours |\n| 4. get the sack | d. to be dismissed from your job |\n| 5. call it a day | e. to stop working for today |\n\n**Zadanie 20.** Przeczytaj tekst, z którego usunięto trzy zdania. Wpisz w luki 1–3 litery A–D. Jedno zdanie nie pasuje do żadnej luki.\n\n> **Working from home**\n>\n> Before 2020, only a small number of people worked from home. **(1)** ______ Today, many companies allow their employees to work remotely for at least part of the week.\n>\n> Remote work has clear advantages. Employees save time and money on commuting, and they can organise their day more flexibly. **(2)** ______ Some people find it hard to separate work from private life, and they end up answering emails late at night.\n>\n> Experts recommend creating a separate space for work at home and sticking to fixed hours. **(3)** ______ It also helps to have regular video meetings, so that workers do not feel isolated from their colleagues.\n\n- A. However, working from home also has its downsides.\n- B. That changed almost overnight when offices closed during the pandemic.\n- C. This way, it is easier to switch off at the end of the day.\n- D. As a result, all offices in Poland have been closed permanently.\n", "key": "**Zadanie 1:** 1. shift · 2. salary · 3. wage · 4. applicant · 5. employer · 6. employee · 7. pension · 8. overtime\n\n**Zadanie 2:** 1. lost · 2. set up · 3. applied · 4. runs · 5. take on · 6. earns · 7. get · 8. went\n\n**Zadanie 3:** 1. estate agent · 2. plumber · 3. surgeon · 4. accountant · 5. receptionist · 6. lawyer\n\n**Zadanie 4:** 1. work · 2. sacked · 3. resign · 4. staff · 5. experience · 6. part-time\n\n**Zadanie 5:** 1. for · 2. on · 3. for · 4. for · 5. in · 6. in\n\n**Zadanie 6:** 1. unemployment · 2. application · 3. professional · 4. demanding · 5. stressful · 6. retirement\n\n**Zadanie 7:** 1. have been looking for a job · 2. was employed / was hired · 3. get this job · 4. has worked / has been working here since · 5. How long have you been working / How long have you worked\n\n**Zadanie 8:** 1. you to read · 2. didn't have to work · 3. told me to stay / made me stay · 4. If she weren't / wasn't so tired · 5. the most hard-working person\n\n**Zadanie 9:** 1. A (*summer job*) · 2. B (*shift* = zmiana) · 3. A (*tips* = napiwki) · 4. C (*deal with*) · 5. A (*earn money*) · 6. B (*every job deserves respect*)\n\n**Zadanie 10:** 1. will be opened / is opening / will open · 2. were laid off · 3. interviewed · 4. be sent · 5. was founded · 6. have been raised\n\n**Zadanie 11:** 1. B · 2. B · 3. A\n\n**Zadanie 12:** 1. F · 2. P · 3. P · 4. F\n\n**Zadanie 13:** 1. if / when · 2. why · 3. out · 4. on · 5. for · 6. at\n\n**Zadanie 14:** 1. B · 2. C · 3. A · 4. D (reakcja E nie pasuje)\n\n**Zadanie 15:** 1. If I had accepted this offer · 2. could / might have been dismissed (fired, sacked) · 3. I have never seen / Never before have I seen · 4. to starting my new job · 5. is said to earn / reportedly earns\n\n**Zadanie 16:** 1. having my computer repaired · 2. denied taking / denied having taken · 3. may have forgotten · 4. has been working here / has been here · 5. isn't necessary\n\n**Zadanie 17:** 1. resignation · 2. conditions · 3. unqualified · 4. promotion · 5. extremely · 6. payment\n\n**Zadanie 18:** 1. A · 2. B · 3. C · 4. A\n\n**Zadanie 19:** 1c · 2a · 3b · 4d · 5e\n\n**Zadanie 20:** 1. B · 2. A · 3. C (zdanie D nie pasuje)\n\n## Słówka z zestawu\n\n| English | Po polsku |\n| --- | --- |\n| salary / wage | pensja (miesięczna) / płaca (tygodniowa, godzinowa) |\n| employer / employee | pracodawca / pracownik |\n| applicant | kandydat |\n| overtime | nadgodziny |\n| shift | zmiana (w pracy) |\n| pension | emerytura |\n| set up / run a business | założyć / prowadzić firmę |\n| take on | zatrudnić |\n| go on strike | zastrajkować |\n| get a promotion | dostać awans |\n| be sacked / get the sack | zostać zwolnionym |\n| resign | zrezygnować, złożyć wypowiedzenie |\n| be made redundant | zostać zwolnionym (redukcja etatów) |\n| lay off | zwolnić (z powodu braku pracy) |\n| be on the dole | być na zasiłku |\n| be in charge of | odpowiadać za, kierować |\n| demanding | wymagający |\n| internship | staż, praktyki |\n| tips | napiwki |\n| round the clock | całą dobę |\n| out of one's depth | ponad czyjeś siły |\n| a dead-end job | praca bez perspektyw |\n| climb the career ladder | piąć się po szczeblach kariery |\n| call it a day | skończyć na dziś |\n"}]};
